<p>
Elia Scotto has a very nice post on the reading habit. He says <a href="https://scotto.me/blog/2026-07-12-how-to-read-more-books/#user-content-fnref-1">he&rsquo;s been reading about a book a week for several years</a>. The gist of the post is &ldquo;how to read more books&rdquo;. His advice for that is straightforward: read when you have nothing else going on. In particular, instead of reaching for your phone and doom scrolling, pick up a book and read a few pages. I agree with all that but not with some of his other recommendations that seem to me to border on the new Luddism.
</p>
<p>
For example, he considers it important to avoid using your phone. That means he wears an analog watch (so he won&rsquo;t have to look at his phone), that he tries to carry a physical book with him at all times, and that when he can&rsquo;t he uses a separate ebook reader.
</p>
<p>
I also read when I have a few spare moments but I do it on my phone. My iPhone is always with me so I don&rsquo;t need to remember to bring along a physical book or separate reader. It&rsquo;s all there on my iPhone. I always read for about an hour when I go to bed and for that I use my iPad. If during the day I find myself with nothing to do, I can always sync my phone to where I left off on the iPad and continue my reading.
</p>
<p>
Really, I don&rsquo;t know why anyone wouldn&rsquo;t do things more or less the way I do. You always have not only your current book but your entire library at hand. But then, I&rsquo;ve never found doom scrolling—or social media, for that matter—attractive so I have nothing to resist.
</p>
<p>
In any event, the important thing is to find time to read. You don&rsquo;t have to be an intellectual or read big, important books. Although I sometimes read books like that, I mostly read mysteries and science fiction. Some of them are as lowbrow as you can get. It doesn&rsquo;t matter. The goal is to read and enjoy the content.
</p>
<p>
Finally, be sure to watch the short video of the late Umberto Eco&rsquo;s library of about 30,000 physical books. Despite what I said above, I love physical books and found it awe inspiring.</p>
